1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,766
Since twenty eighteen, about twenty-one million cars in the U.S.

2
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:06,221
have been recalled for backup camera problems.

3
00:00:06,271 --> 00:00:08,623
Most of the time, the camera itself was fine.

4
00:00:08,673 --> 00:00:11,078
The computer in the middle of the car was not.

5
00:00:14,128 --> 00:00:20,885
Today one computer runs the instrument cluster, the rear camera, infotainment, software updates, and now an AI assistant.

6
00:00:20,935 --> 00:00:21,335
The U.S.

7
00:00:21,385 --> 00:00:26,510
safety standard says the rear image must appear within two seconds of shifting into reverse.

8
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:32,848
This is our car computer, running QNX 8.0 in QEMU.

9
00:00:32,898 --> 00:00:36,052
Ten processes, each isolated by the microkernel.

10
00:00:36,102 --> 00:00:40,258
The rear camera is its own process, with its own window on top.

11
00:00:40,308 --> 00:00:46,934
It waits on the vehicle bus, a QNX resource manager, and gets its reply the moment the gear changes.

12
00:00:49,984 --> 00:00:51,835
We built the same code two ways.

13
00:00:51,885 --> 00:00:57,062
On the left, the camera lives inside infotainment, the way many recalled cars are built.

14
00:00:57,112 --> 00:00:59,914
On the right, the camera is its own QNX process.

15
00:00:59,964 --> 00:01:05,438
A separate probe reads the screen every four milliseconds, like a person filming the display.

16
00:01:08,488 --> 00:01:10,536
Scene one follows the federal test.

17
00:01:10,586 --> 00:01:15,273
Everything starts from off, and the driver shifts into reverse at four seconds.

18
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:18,990
On the left, the camera has to wait for infotainment to start.

19
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:20,669
Two point three six seconds.

20
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:21,329
That fails.

21
00:01:21,379 --> 00:01:25,166
On the right, the image is on screen in about five milliseconds.

22
00:01:28,216 --> 00:01:28,743
Scene two.

23
00:01:28,793 --> 00:01:33,416
Overnight, while the car charges, an update installs a broken infotainment build.

24
00:01:33,466 --> 00:01:37,628
It crashes three times, and the supervisor rolls back to the old version.

25
00:01:37,678 --> 00:01:40,166
The charging session never misses a request.

26
00:01:43,216 --> 00:01:43,868
Scene three.

27
00:01:43,918 --> 00:01:48,486
In the morning the car wakes up, and the driver shifts into reverse right away.

28
00:01:48,536 --> 00:01:52,696
On the left, the camera never appears during the whole backing maneuver.

29
00:01:52,746 --> 00:01:55,502
On the right, it is there in three milliseconds.

30
00:01:58,552 --> 00:01:59,186
Scene four.

31
00:01:59,236 --> 00:02:03,976
The AI assistant keeps every CPU busy, and reverse still works on both sides.

32
00:02:04,026 --> 00:02:06,900
Then we kill infotainment with kill minus nine.

33
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:09,015
On the left, the camera goes dark.

34
00:02:09,065 --> 00:02:11,566
On the right, only infotainment restarts.

35
00:02:11,616 --> 00:02:14,054
The camera and the cluster keep running.

36
00:02:17,104 --> 00:02:19,068
Same car, same CPUs, same code.

37
00:02:19,118 --> 00:02:22,056
The only difference is where the camera lives.

38
00:02:22,106 --> 00:02:30,110
With QNX process isolation, the rear image no longer depends on the biggest, slowest, most often updated program in the car.

39
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:33,819
RearGuard.

40
00:02:33,869 --> 00:02:36,799
Everything you saw ran on QNX 8.0 in QEMU.

41
00:02:36,849 --> 00:02:41,198
The code, the logs, and the build steps are in the repository.
